# Lokutara compliance gate

For every feature, identify personal data, purpose, recipient, retention, legal
basis/consent, user control, access role, and logging before implementation.

- Collect only fields needed for the stated purpose. Never place assessment
  answers, counselling details, diagnoses, or community bodies in analytics or
  general application logs.
- Present a plain-language notice at collection. Record the accepted notice
  version and timestamp where consent or acknowledgement is required.
- Keep the service adult-only unless verified parent/guardian consent and a
  child-specific legal review are implemented.
- Assessment outputs are developmental, non-diagnostic, and never the sole
  basis for hiring, firing, promotion, discipline, compensation, treatment, or
  another consequential decision. Require human review and accommodations.
- Keep counselling content separate from employer reporting, analytics,
  community, leads, and general product administration.
- Require least-privilege authorization for every private record. Test
  cross-account access and admin redaction.
- Support access/export, correction, consent withdrawal, grievance, and
  erasure. Preserve only statutory, security, dispute, or legal-hold records
  and document why.
- User-generated content needs privacy warnings, rules, complaint intake,
  moderation, and lawful preservation/takedown handling.
- Show the full GST-inclusive price, seller identity, delivery, cancellation,
  refund, and grievance information before payment. Never store card/UPI
  credentials.
- New processors or cross-border transfers require a vendor review, contract,
  location, subprocessor, deletion, breach, and security entry.
- Security changes must preserve same-origin mutation checks, secure cookies,
  rate limits, input limits, auditability, incident response, and CERT-In log
  and reporting duties.
- Update `lib/legal/compliance.ts`, public notices, tests, and
  `docs/compliance/` whenever data use, vendors, claims, or retention changes.
- Do not claim “compliant”, “validated”, “licensed psychometric”, “clinical”,
  or “confidential” without evidence and owner/counsel approval.
